  "ballot": {
    "album": "Coleman Hawkins \u2014 The Genius of Coleman Hawkins (1957)",
    "scope_check": {
      "in_window": true,
      "boundary_note": "The 1957 date is within the year window and admissible, but its classification as hard-bop is debatable: it may be better understood as late swing and mainstream standards artistry that bridges into post-bebop rather than as a quintessential hard-bop statement."
    },
    "tier": "contested",
    "priority": "consider",
    "case_for": "This is a major leader date by the foundational tenor saxophonist, giving Hawkins a substantial solo statement rather than only his co-billed appearance on Encounters Ben Webster. The session has strong discovery and session-gravity value: Hawkins is heard in mature form with Oscar Peterson, Herb Ellis, Ray Brown, and Alvin Stoller, while the standards provide a clear demonstration of the harmonic, tonal, and ballad vocabulary inherited by Rollins, Coltrane, and later tenorists (dossier S1, S5). AllMusic rates it four stars and the dossier reports a 3.5-star Penguin Guide assessment, though the Penguin claim is not directly sourced in the evidence supplied.",
    "case_against": "The album may be an excellent conventional standards blowing date rather than Hawkins's indispensable postwar statement: its twelve brief treatments overlap heavily in style and personnel with the more historically distinctive Encounters Ben Webster, recorded only eight days earlier. The stronger claim that this is Hawkins's defining Verve-era date is also challenged by The Hawk Flies High, often treated as the more definitive late-career leader session. Actual dossier sourcing is thin for a canon decision\u2014largely one substantive AllMusic source plus Wikipedia and metadata sources\u2014and the incomplete track assignments further limit confidence in the documentation.",
    "disagreement": "The references agree that the album is canon-worthy enough to remain contested, but split on urgency: Reference 1 calls it strong priority, while Reference 2 calls it consider because of its conventionality and shadowed status beside Encounters Ben Webster. They also differ on scope emphasis: Reference 1 sees an unambiguous hard-bop fit, while Reference 2 regards the late-swing/mainstream character as a meaningful style-boundary question.",
    "recommendation": "Keep the album in consideration as a contested, moderate-priority Hawkins leader date, but ask John to compare it directly with The Hawk Flies High and Encounters Ben Webster before deciding.",
    "confidence": "medium"
  }
}